  "raw": {
    "https://careers.aut.ac.nz/jobdetails?ajid=hfM6e": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Research Fellow - R13200.01 - AUT Students Staff Alumni AUT Ako Study Tawāhi International Oranga Tauira Student\n                                            Life Rangahau Research Whanaukataka Partnerships Mō Mātou About Ako Study Tāwāhi International Oranga Tauira Student Life Rangahau Research Ngātahi Partnerships Mō\n                                Mātou About Sign in Sign in Home About Careers at AUT Job vacancies Research Fellow - R13200.01 Research Fellow - R13200.01 Seeking expertise in researching novel methods to measure fluid transport in trees Work under a variety of experts and in collaboration with institutions in New Zealand and overseas. 1.5-2 year fixed-term, full-time based at AUT's City Campus in Auckland Auckland University of Technology (AUT) is New Zealand's newest university and one of its most distinctive. Centred on students and grounded in knowledge that works , AUT is 5-star rated by QS, with more than 27,000 students and 4,000 staff based primarily across three Auckland campuses. It's a place of real opportunity, where the values of Pono, Tika and Aroha shape how talented, progressive people work together – and you'll feel that in the culture, in the collaboration and in the difference the work makes for students, communities and the world. Our community is stronger because of the role Te Tiriti plays in how we operate, and because Māori and Pacific knowledge, leadership and contribution are central to who we are. We embrace diversity in all its forms and are proud to welcome people of all ethnicities, genders, sexual orientations, religious and political beliefs, socio-economic situations and accessibility needs. AUT's Earth & Life Sciences department is part of our School of Science . The School comprises environmental and marine science, biological science, biomedical science, medical diagnostics, psychology and neuroscience, chemistry and food science. Science plays a pivotal role in AUT’s place as a university of technology and we are actively working to promote its profile within the university. We have a wonderful community of deeply committed academic scholars and professional colleagues, guided by Te Aronui (AUT's Te Tiriti framework). Our school produces culturally aware graduates with practical experience and skills that work in the real world, not just on paper, through courses designed to meet the shifting needs of the science workforce. Our staff drive innovative progress through our intensive, productive world-class research and engaging, relevant and practical teaching. Energy is high and it is an exciting time to consider joining us. The School is part of our dynamic Faculty of Health and Environmental Sciences . The Opportunity | Te Whiwhinga mahi AUT’s School of Science and School of Engineering, Computer, and Mathematical Sciences are looking for a highly motivated research fellow (1.0 FTE, 1.5-2 years) for a highly interdisciplinary project on researching novel methods to measure fluid transport in trees. The candidate should have a solid background in one of the following three fields, with a keen interest to become an expert in the other two: electrical engineering and sensor development, plant ecophysiology and environmental measurements, neural networks and/or finite element models. Expected are excellent English language skills, advanced programming skills (Matlab or R), preparedness to quickly pick up skills that may be outside the candidate’s background, readiness to travel within New Zealand, and work under a variety of experts and in collaboration with institutions in New Zealand and overseas. What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou At AUT, you’ll join a values-led university that supports you to thrive . Benefit from flexible working, a supportive culture, cultural capability development for all staff, and strong growth opportunities – while advancing your impact through clear career pathways, recognition and research and teaching support. Learn more about our competitive benefits tailored to all staff https://www.aut.ac.nz/about/careers-at-aut/working-at-aut/staff-benefits . About you | Ko wai koe Along with your full academic CV - please also submit a Cover Letter explaining why this particular role (and AUT) is of interest, how your experience aligns with the key selection criteria we're seeking below A PhD or equivalent in electronics, sensor development, applied physics, or a closely related engineering discipline A strong background in one of the following fields: a) ecophysiology or environmental measuring techniques, b) electrical engineering and sensor development, or c) neural networks/finite element models, with a keen interest to quickly learn in the other two fields Strong programming skills (Matlab/R or equivalent) All applications must be submitted through the online application process. Further Information | Pārongo Anō Start Date: February/March 2027 (to be agreed with the appointee). Salary Ranges | Ngā Utu: $81,000 - $115,000 per annum (appointment level and salary commensurate with skills and experience) For academic enquiries: Contact David Wilson, Associate Professor, david.wilson@aut.ac.nz Closing Date | Te rā aukati: 2 November 2026, 11:55pm Job Reference | Tohutoro Mahi: 96493 At AUT we strive to be a place where people love to work and learn. We are committed to te Tiriti, excellence and inclusivity and aspire to be the University of choice for Māori and Pacific communities. We welcome people of all ethnicities, genders, sexual orientations, religious and political beliefs, socio-economic situations and accessibility needs. Please note that all applications must be submitted through the online application process. For further information, please send us an enquiry here or call 921 9499 . Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email. You will need to apply through the standard registration process. Nā tō rourou, nā taku rourou ka ora ai te iwi. With your food basket and my food basket, the people will thrive. Job Details Reference # 96493 Location (s) City Campus Employment type (s) Fixed term Closes on 02 Nov 2026 23:55 Expertise Research Experience level (s) Experienced (Less than 2 years) More details (document) PD 11023 - Research Fellow (electronics and tree physiology).pdf Posted on 24 Aug 2026 Apply Email this job to a friend Print CONTACT US 0800 AUT UNI (0800 288 864) Outside NZ: +64 9 921 9999 Contact us\n                                online AUT CITY CAMPUS 55 Wellesley Street East, Auckland Central Campus map [PDF, 309.7 KB] AUT NORTH CAMPUS 90 Akoranga Drive, Northcote, Auckland Campus map [PDF, 166.7 KB] AUT SOUTH CAMPUS 640 Great South Road, Manukau, Auckland Campus map [PDF, 207.5 KB] Arion My AUT Canvas Library Careers Copyright Privacy Site map Student\n                                    feedback: complaints, suggestions and compliments Facebook LinkedIn TikTok Douyin Youtube Instagram WeChat Weibo XiaoHongShu This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either\n            doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your\n            Administrator for further assistance."
